[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105476,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipofibromatosis (LPF) is a rare paediatric soft tissue tumour with a distinctive admixture of mature fat, fascicles of bland spindled cells, and lipoblast-like cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105477,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8851/1 Lipofibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105478,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FB51.Y &amp; XH4QB6 Other specified fibroblastic disorders &amp; Lipofibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105479,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> infantile/juvenile fibromatosis (non-desmoid type).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105480,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105481,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF involves the hands and feet, although it can occur in other locations {11075850}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105482,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF typically manifests as a slowly growing subcutaneous mass {11075850}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105483,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF occurs more often in boys than in girls, with a wide age range (birth to 14&nbsp;years). As many as 20% of reported cases have been congenital {11075850; 30310176}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105484,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105485,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF may represent a morphological pattern common to a variety of locally recurring paediatric tumours of the extremities, rather than a single specific entity. The presence of <em>FN1</em>::<em>EGF</em> or <em>FN1</em>::<em>TGFA</em> fusions suggests that some cases probably represent early or non-calcified calcifying aponeurotic fibromas. Moreover, identification of fusions involving genes encoding EGFR (HER1) ligands (<em>EGF</em>, <em>HBEGF</em>, <em>TGFA</em>) or <em>EGFR</em> itself, or genes encoding other receptor tyrosine kinases (<em>ROS1</em>, <em>RET</em>, <em>PDGFRB</em>), suggests that activation of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway might be implicated in the pathogenesis of LPF {30310176}.</p>\r\n<p>Cytogenetic abnormalities have been described in one case showing a translocation {18036401}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105486,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The firm, rubbery, poorly demarcated mass has a white or yellow cut surface. Most are 10&ndash;70&nbsp;mm in diameter, although very large tumours involving the entire extremity have been reported {11075850; 30310176; 19461290}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105487,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF is composed of fascicles of mature fibrous tissue with bland, uniform, elongated nuclei and eosinophilic cytoplasm traversing mature adipose tissue. The adipose tissue may form a major proportion of the lesion. In newborn patients, the fat lobules may have an immature appearance with a myxoid matrix. Small collections of univacuolated cells may be present in the fibroblastic component, where melanin-laden cells have been occasionally documented. LPF entraps skeletal muscle and subcutaneous structures. Mitoses are rare and necrosis is absent. Immunohistochemically, the fibroblasts show variable expression of CD34 and SMA, and the fat is S100-positive. The uncommon pigmented cells express melanocytic markers {11075850; 18236073}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105488,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105489,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Detection of the <em>FN1</em>::<em>EGF</em> gene fusion may be helpful in cases of calcifying aponeurotic fibromas lacking calcification {30310176}, although this is not required for routine diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105490,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> histology showing an admixture of mature fat, short fascicles of bland spindled cells, and lipoblast-like cells; exclusion of other entities with an LPF-like pattern (e.g. calcifying aponeurotic fibroma, infantile fibrosarcoma).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105491,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105492,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 485,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF has a high rate of local recurrence but no metastatic potential. Congenital onset, male sex, acral location, mitotic activity in the fibroblastic component, and incomplete excision may be risk factors for recurrence {11075850}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:28:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]